    "body": "## Problem\n\nEvery released KV block goes straight back to the free queue. A repeated prefix — a resumed agent turn, a shared system prompt, a retried request — is therefore recomputed from scratch even though its KV is still sitting in memory.\n\n## Change\n\nBlocks that still carry a hash when released stay attached to the prefix-cache hash table and are kept in a **warm table**. A later request with the same prefix hits them directly and skips the recompute.\n\nWhen free blocks run short, the warm table is evicted under pressure-gated retention (cold → hot):\n\n| usage | retention |\n|---|---|\n| < 25% | 12h |\n| < 35% | 6h |\n| < 50% | 3h |\n| < 65% | 1h |\n| < 80% | 30m |\n| ≥ 80% | reclaim immediately |\n\nA block touched on every hit leaves the warm table, is re-stamped when released again, and moves to the back of the queue — so hot blocks survive many eviction rounds while cold ones age out from the head. Eviction clears the hash too, so a demoted block can never be hit again.\n\n## Two things that are easy to get wrong\n\n**`get_num_free_blocks` must count warm blocks — on both return paths.**\n\nThe scheduler uses that number to decide whether a request can be promoted. A short count makes it believe the pool is full: nothing is ever promoted and the engine stalls with `waiting > 0` and an idle GPU. This is the fingerprint to watch for if the change is ever ported somewhere the accounting differs.\n\n**The warm table is capped at half the pool.**\n\nWithout a cap, a long run of one-shot prefixes grows it without bound until genuinely free blocks are crowded out.\n\nThe table is per kv-cache group and respects the partitioned queue's `block_id` ranges, so the state group and the data group never see each other's blocks.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Repeated request reports `cache_read=3712` — the prefix is hit, not recomputed.\n- 4-way concurrency over 30 minutes: no stall, `waiting == 0`.\n- Retention tiers verified by driving usage through each band and observing evictions at the expected age.",
